  "reviewedHead": "fd0df6aa4596a0fa15b4ca1983d47cc69697260c",
  "summary": "PR1809 introduces a well-structured health endpoint authentication boundary. The prior first-review blocking finding — that FeedHealthPanel.fetchSidecarHealth() never sends an Authorization header — is a false positive upon independent reassessment with the full runtime/bootstrap evidence. main.ts:282 calls installRuntimeFetchPatch() synchronously before new App() at line 343, which transitively constructs FeedHealthPanel at panel-layout.ts:2310 inside the async app.init(). The runtime patch installed by createRuntimeFetch intercepts all window.fetch calls: getApiTargetFromRequestInput normalizes 'http://127.0.0.1:<port>/api/health' (the panel's absolute URL) to '/api/health' because 127.0.0.1 is in APP_HOSTS, and the router then resolves the confirmed native port, obtains the cached IPC token, and sets Authorization: Bearer <token> before forwarding. The runtime-fetch-fallback tests (29/29 pass) confirm this injection path. No actual bearer injection gap exists in production desktop mode; the panel degrades gracefully to diagnosticsPending during the brief window before IPC token availability, which is the intended design. All other changed files are correct: the sidecar's SidecarHealth.project correctly withholds inventory from unauthenticated callers and never evaluates the readDetails callback for them; summarizeSidecarHealth properly distinguishes public liveness from authenticated diagnostics; SidecarRuntimeFiles enforces strict port validation; checkup.mjs and smoke.mts use shared discovery without hardcoded defaults; intel-expansion-cluster3 tests correctly add auth headers to /api/health calls that assert on feed-level inventory; live-diagnostics-snapshot.ts correctly gates authenticated on ok && obj.authenticated === true; and the health-contract suite (47+34 inner tests, 0 fail) provides concrete isolation coverage. One nonblocking documentation note is raised: FeedHealthPanel.fetchSidecarHealth relies implicitly on the runtime fetch patch for bearer injection but carries no comment indicating this dependency, which could confuse future maintainers reading the component in isolation.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "nonblocking",
      "path": "src/components/FeedHealthPanel.ts",
      "line": 107,
      "message": "fetchSidecarHealth() issues a bare fetch with only { Accept: 'application/json' } — no Authorization header is set in the component itself. Bearer injection relies entirely on the global runtime fetch patch (installRuntimeFetchPatch, main.ts:282) being installed before this component is constructed. This is correct in the current bootstrap order, but the implicit dependency is undocumented. A comment noting that auth header injection is provided by the runtime fetch patch would prevent a future developer reading this component in isolation from misreading it as an unauthenticated call or inadvertently breaking the ordering invariant."
    }
